# Paths exempt from LAYER 3 ONLY — the "does this look like a credential" scan.
#
# Layers 1 and 2 are NOT affected and cannot be opted out of: you can never
# commit .env, and you can never commit a real value from it. That is deliberate.
# A bypass that reaches the layer which catches actual secrets is not a bypass,
# it is a hole.
#
# One git pathspec per line.

# A secret-scanner's own tests must contain credential-shaped strings — that is
# what they are for. Without this the package can never test its own patterns.
packages/secret-scan/test/*

# @broberg/mail's webhook test pins Svix's OWN PUBLISHED test vector — the pair
# they document as the correct answer (svix/svix-webhooks,
# javascript/src/webhook.test.ts, "sign function works"). Its whsec_ prefix is
# shared with Stripe, so layer 3 reads it as a real credential and refuses the
# commit. It cannot know this one is documentation.
#
# Exempting ONE FILE by exact path, not the directory: this is the same reason
# the scanner's own fixtures are exempt — a signature test must contain something
# credential-shaped or it tests nothing. A wider pattern would silence the file
# next to it too.
#
# The alternative was assembling the secret from fragments at runtime, as the
# pre-commit gate's own test does. Rejected here on purpose: the value of a
# pinned vector is that a reader can compare it to Svix's page BYTE FOR BYTE,
# and splitting it up destroys exactly that.
packages/mail/test/webhook.test.ts
